      "type": "thinking",
      "thinking": "Let me analyze this fragment of an OIML certificate R49/2006-DE1-14.01 (water meters).\n\nThis fragment covers pages 9-11 of 11. It contains multiple model variants:\n\n1. **ET1, ET3, ET4, DN15, Q₃ 1,6 m³/h** — flow ranges with 3 variants (Q₃/Q₁ = 20, 25, 31.5), orientation: rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° and V 45°\n2. **ET2, DN15, Q₃ 1,6 m³/h** — 7 variants (Q₃/Q₁ = 16, 20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63), orientation: H\n3. **ET2, DN15, Q₃ 1,6 m³/h** (repeated header but likely ET1, ET3, ET4 based on earlier pattern — but as shown it says ET2; I should extract what's there) — 2 variants (Q₃/Q₁ = 16, 20), orientation: rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° und V 45°\n4. **ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q₃ 2,5 m³/h** — 6 variants (Q₃/Q₁ = 16, 20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50), orientation: H\n5. **ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q₃ 2,5 m³/h** — 3 variants (Q₃/Q₁ = 16, 20, 25), orientation: rising/falling pipe V90/V45\n6. **ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q₃ 4,0 m³/h** — 6 variants (Q₃/Q₁ = 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80), orientation: H\n7. **ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q₃ 4,0 m³/h** — 3 variants (Q₃/Q₁ = 25, 31.5, 40), orientation: rising/falling\n\nPlus pressure range and pressure loss table:\n- Pmin: 0.3 bar (0.03 MPa)\n- Pmax: 10 bar (1.0 MPa)\n- DN15 Q3 1.6: ΔP 25\n- DN15 Q3 2.5: ΔP 63 — wait, that's odd, DN15 Q3 2.5 m³/h appears in pressure table but not in flow tables above. Actually this fragment doesn't show DN15 Q3 2.5 flow tables — it may have been in part 2. I should just record what's in this fragment.\n- DN20 Q3 2.5: ΔP 40\n- DN20 Q3 4.0: ΔP 63\n\nMinimum straight length of inlet/outlet pipe: 0 mm / 0 mm.\n\nAlso:\n- Accuracy class: ±3% (Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4), ±5% (Q1 ≤ Q < Q2)\n- Water temperature range: 30°C to 90°C\n\nCertificate info:\n- number: R49/2006-DE1-14.01\n- issuing authority: Physikalisch-Technische Bundesanstalt\n- page_total: 11\n\nThe recommendation: R49 (Continuous totalizing systems of liquid other than water... actually R49 is Water meters intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water). Edition 2006 presumably (from cert number R49/2006-DE1-14.01). I could set recommendation id \"R49\", edition 2006. The cert number includes \"2006\" which suggests the edition. It's reasonable but let me be careful about fabricating. The scheme in number \"R49/2006-DE1-14.01\" — DE1 is the PTB scheme. The rule says extract from the content present. The cert number clearly indicates R49 and 2006. I'll include recommendation: {id: \"R49\", edition: 2006, scheme: \"DE1\"} — hmm, scheme field for recommendation is the certificate scheme? In the certificate layer, \"scheme (A|B)\". The cert number R49/2006-DE1-14.01: DE1 likely denotes issuing branch. Actually in OIML-CS, scheme is A or B. The certificate number format is R49/2006-DE1-14.01 where DE1 is the country/authority code and 14.01 is sequential. I shouldn't guess A|B. I'll omit scheme.\n\nAccuracy classes: for R49 water meters, accuracy classes... The accuracy class here is expressed as ±3% / ±5% which is metrological, but R49 defines accuracy classes 1 and 2. The fragment only gives ±3%/±5%. I'll put accuracy_class... The schema has recommendation.accuracy_classes[]. Hmm, ±3/±5% are maximum permissible errors, not classes per se. But I could record accuracy class as characteristics config_level: attribute \"maximum_permitted_error\" varying by flow region. Let me record in characteristics.\n\nNow how to model this per the layered schema:\n\nmodel_family models: The models are combinations of type (ET1/ET2/ET3/ET4), DN size, Q3 value, and Q3/Q1 ratio (R value), and orientation. That's a big matrix.\n\nA pragmatic approach: define models per (type-set, DN, Q3, R) combination? That would be many. Alternatively define models per meter designation: e.g. \"ET1-DN15-Q3_1.6-R20-V\" etc. That's complex.\n\nSimpler: use matrix_tables to preserve the flow range tables, and use config_level or model_level for key attributes.\n\nLet me think about what the cert actually covers:\n\n- DN15, Q3=1.6 m³/h: types ET1, ET3, ET4 (V orientations: V90/V45, rising/falling) with R ∈ {20, 25, 31.5}; type ET2 (H orientation) with R ∈ {16, 20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63}; and a third table labeled \"ET2, DN15\" but with rising/falling orientation and R ∈ {16, 20} — this is likely a typo in OCR or the original for ET1/ET3/ET4. Hmm. Given the second ET2 DN15 entry duplicates R=16,20 from the H table but with vertical orientation, it likely means ET1, ET3, ET4 (the vertical variants) with R=16 and 20. But the markdown says ET2. I must not fabricate; I'll transcribe as given.\n\n- DN20, Q3=2.5 m³/h: types ET1-ET4, H orientation, R ∈ {16, 20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50}; and V orientation, R ∈ {16, 20, 25}.\n- DN20, Q3=4.0 m³/h: types ET1-ET4, H orientation, R ∈ {25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80}; V orientation, R ∈ {25, 31.5, 40}.\n\nModel designations: the certified type designations are ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4. DN15/DN20 are sizes, Q3 is flow.\n\nApproach: \n- type_designations: [\"ET1\", \"ET2\", \"ET3\", \"ET4\"]\n- model_family: family_name null, models: list each distinct model configuration. I could enumerate models as combinations: e.g., model_id \"DN15-Q3_1.6-R20-vertical\" etc. But that's a lot and mixes types.\n\nAlternative pragmatic approach: treat each \"heading\" (DN, Q3, orientation group) as a model, with attributes including applicable types. Then use config_level for the R (Q3/Q1 ratio) axis within each model.\n\nActually the cleanest way given the schema: \n\nmodel_family.models[] with model_id like \"ET1/ET3/ET4 DN15 Q3=1.6 V-orientation\", attributes {dn: 15, q3: 1.6, ...}. And matrix_tables preserving each flow range table with rows per R variant.\n\nLet me design matrix_tables entries: one per heading, each with columns [q1, q2, q3, q4, q2_over_q1, q3_over_q1], rows per variant (condition = R value or index). Each row is a variant.\n\nThen characteristics.config_level with attribute q1, q2, q3, q4 varying along axis \"q3/q1 ratio\" per model? That gets heavy. Perhaps config_level entries per model:\n\nFor model \"DN15 Q3 1.6 m³/h (ET1/ET3/ET4, vertical)\":\n- config_level: attribute \"q1\", axis \"q3/q1 ratio\", values: [{condition: \"R=20\", value: 0.08}, {condition: \"R=25\", value: 0.06}, {condition: \"R=31.5\", value: 0.05}] — but config_level values shape is {condition, value} plus unit_symbol presumably per the schema: `values[]: {condition, value}`. Rule 5 says values follow same structured form: {condition, value, unit_symbol}.\n\nHmm, this is getting big. Let me decide scope: This is part 3 of 3, a fragment. The instruction: \"Return one JSON object conforming to the layered schema for the content present in THIS fragment only.\"\n\nI'll produce:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2006-DE1-14.01\",\n    \"page_total\": 11\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"Physikalisch-Technische Bundesanstalt\"\n  },\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"ET1\", \"ET2\", \"ET3\", \"ET4\"],\n    \"model_designations\": ... // not in schema\n  },\n  ...\n}\n```\n\nCategory: R49 = water meters. Category \"Water meter\". Q3 1.6 m³/h indicates water meter. Yes.\n\nmodel_family: models — I'll enumerate each heading as a model variant. There are 7 headings:\n\n1. ET1, ET3, ET4, DN15, Q3 1.6 m³/h — vertical orientations (V90/V45, rising/falling) — 3 R variants\n2. ET2, DN15, Q3 1.6 m³/h — H — 7 R variants\n3. ET2 (as printed), DN15, Q3 1.6 m³/h — vertical — 2 R variants\n4. ET1-ET4, DN20, Q3 2.5 m³/h — H — 6 R variants\n5. ET1-ET4, DN20, Q3 2.5 m³/h — vertical — 3 R variants\n6. ET1-ET4, DN20, Q3 4.0 m³/h — H — 6 R variants\n7. ET1-ET4, DN20, Q3 4.0 m³/h — vertical — 3 R variants\n\nModel IDs: e.g. \"DN15-Q3_1.6-H\" and \"DN15-Q3_1.6-V\" with types listed. But heading 1 and 3 differ in types (1: ET1/ET3/ET4; 3: ET2 as printed). Hmm.\n\nSimplify: model_id combining types + DN + Q3 + orientation:\n- \"ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3_1.6_V\" (vertical)\n- \"ET2_DN15_Q3_1.6_H\"\n- \"ET2_DN15_Q3_1.6_V\" (as printed)\n- \"ET1-ET4_DN20_Q3_2.5_H\"\n- \"ET1-ET4_DN20_Q3_2.5_V\"\n- \"ET1-ET4_DN20_Q3_4.0_H\"\n- \"ET1-ET4_DN20_Q3_4.0_V\"\n\nAttributes per model: types, dn, q3, orientation.\n\nThen matrix_tables: one per heading, name like \"Flow range – DN15 Q3 1.6 m³/h (ET1/ET3/ET4, vertical)\", columns [\"variant\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q2_over_q1\", \"q3_over_q1\"], rows per variant with condition being the R ratio or index.\n\nActually to keep the rows keyed, I'll use \"q3_over_q1\" as the key in rows.\n\nAlso characteristics.type_level: water temperature range 30-90 °C, accuracy class errors, pressure range (min 0.3 bar, max 10 bar), minimum straight length inlet 0 mm / outlet 0 mm.\n\ncharacteristics.model_level: q3 per model (1.6, 2.5, 4.0), dn per model. And pressure loss ΔP per model (DN15 Q3 1.6: 25; DN15 Q3 2.5: 63; DN20 Q3 2.5: 40; DN20 Q3 4.0: 63).\n\nHmm wait — ΔP values: \"ΔP 25\", \"ΔP 63\" — these are pressure loss classes (ΔP in bar × 10^5? Actually in water meters, Δp class like Δp 25 means max pressure loss 0.25 bar? In OIML R49 / EN 14154, pressure loss classes are Δp 25, 40, 63 corresponding to 0.25 bar, 0.4 bar, 0.63 bar). I'll record as string \"ΔP 25\" or value 25 with attribute \"pressure_loss_class\". I'll record as value string \"ΔP 25\"? Better: attribute \"pressure_loss_class\", value 25, no unit (it's a class designation). Or keep as string \"25\". I'll use value: 25, unit null, attribute \"pressure_loss_class_delta_p\".\n\nNow the pressure table: Pmin 0.3 bar (0.03 MPa), Pmax 10 bar (1.0 MPa). These are type_level characteristics: minimum_pressure 0.3 bar, maximum_pressure 10 bar.\n\nAccuracy: config_level with axis \"flow region\":\n- attribute \"maximum_permitted_error\", axis \"flow_region\", values: [{condition: \"Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4\", value: 3, unit_symbol: \"%\"}, {condition: \"Q1 ≤ Q < Q2\", value: 5, unit_symbol: \"%\"}]\n\nWater temperature: type_level: water_temperature_range {min:30, max:90} °C.\n\nNote: temperature 30 °C to 90 °C — hot water meter. OK.\n\nFootnotes: none present.\n\nTest reports: none in this fragment.\n\nRevision history: none.\n\nApplicants/manufacturers: not in this fragment (part 3). Omit.\n\nDate issued: not present. Omit.\n\nScheme: not determinable; omit. project_number: not present. member_state: not explicitly stated (PTB is Germany; \"DE1\" in the number suggests Germany). Could set member_state \"Germany\"? It's inferable but not stated. I'll omit to be safe. Actually oiml_issuer_id not present either.\n\ncertificate.page_total: 11 (from \"Page 9 of 11 pages\"). Include.\n\nNow let me carefully extract the tables.\n\n**Table 1: ET1, ET3, ET4, DN15, Q3 1,6 m³/h**\n- Q1: 0.08, 0.06, 0.05\n- Q2: 0.13, 0.10, 0.08\n- Q3: 1.6 (all)\n- Q4: 2.00 (all)\n- Q2/Q1: 1.6 (all)\n- Q3/Q1: 20, 25, 31.5\n\n**Table 2: ET2, DN15, Q3 1,6 m³/h**\n- Q1: 0.100, 0.080, 0.064, 0.051, 0.040, 0.032, 0.025\n- Q2: 0.160, 0.128, 0.102, 0.081, 0.064, 0.051, 0.041\n- Q3: 1.6 ×7\n- Q4: 2.00 ×7\n- Q2/Q1: 1.6 ×7\n- Q3/Q1: 16, 20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63\n\n**Table 3: ET2, DN15, Q3 1,6 m³/h (vertical)**\n- Q1: 0.100, 0.080\n- Q2: 0.160, 0.128\n- Q3: 1.6, 1.6\n- Q4: 2.00, 2.00\n- Q2/Q1: 1.6, 1.6\n- Q3/Q1: 16, 20\n\n**Table 4: ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q3 2,5 m³/h (H)**\n- Q1: 0.16, 0.13, 0.10, 0.08, 0.06, 0.050\n- Q2: 0.25, 0.20, 0.16, 0.13, 0.10, 0.080\n- Q3: 2.5 ×6\n- Q4: 3.13 ×6\n- Q2/Q1: 1.6 ×6\n- Q3/Q1: 16, 20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50\n\n**Table 5: ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q3 2,5 m³/h (vertical)**\n- Q1: 0.16, 0.13, 0.10\n- Q2: 0.25, 0.20, 0.16\n- Q3: 2.5 ×3\n- Q4: 3.13 ×3\n- Q2/Q1: 1.6 ×3\n- Q3/Q1: 16, 20, 25\n\n**Table 6: ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q3 4,0 m³/h (H)**\n- Q1: 0.16, 0.13, 0.10, 0.08, 0.06, 0.05\n- Q2: 0.26, 0.20, 0.16, 0.13, 0.10, 0.08\n- Q3: 4.0 ×6\n- Q4: 5.00 ×6\n- Q2/Q1: 1.6 ×6\n- Q3/Q1: 25, 31.5, 40, 50, 63, 80\n\n**Table 7: ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN20, Q3 4,0 m³/h (vertical)**\n- Q1: 0.16, 0.13, 0.10\n- Q2: 0.26, 0.20, 0.16\n- Q3: 4.0 ×3\n- Q4: 5.00 ×3\n- Q2/Q1: 1.6 ×3\n- Q3/Q1: 25, 31.5, 40\n\n**Pressure table:**\n- Pmin: 0.3 bar (0.03 MPa)\n- Pmax: 10 bar (1.0 MPa)\n- DN15 Q3 1.6: ΔP 25\n- DN15 Q3 2.5: ΔP 63\n- DN20 Q3 2.5: ΔP 40\n- DN20 Q3 4.0: ΔP 63\n\nNow let me structure the JSON. I'll go with model_family with 7 models as described.\n\nFor characteristics.model_level, I could add:\n- nominal_diameter (dn) per model\n- q3 per model\n- maximum_permissible_error — same for all, so type/config level instead\n- orientation per model (H vs V)\n- pressure_loss_class per model — but the pressure table lists by DN/Q3 combos, not by orientation. DN15 Q3 1.6 covers models 1,2,3 → ΔP 25. I could list per model: models \"ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3_1.6_V\", \"ET2_DN15_Q3_1.6_H\", \"ET2_DN15_Q3_1.6_V\" all get 25.\n\nmodel_level entries:\n1. attribute \"nominal_diameter\", unit \"mm\", values: 15 for the three DN15 models, 20 for the four DN20 models.\n2. attribute \"q3\" (permanent flow rate), unit \"m³/h\", values: 1.6/1.6/1.6/2.5/2.5/4.0/4.0.\n3. attribute \"pressure_loss_class_delta_p\": values 25 (DN15 Q3 1.6 models), 63 for \"DN15 Q3 2.5\" — wait, there's no DN15 Q3 2.5 model in my model list because this fragment has no flow table for it. The pressure table references DN15 Q3 2.5 m³/h. Since this fragment must stand alone, I should record the pressure table as a matrix_table including that row, and in model_level pressure loss I can include a value for condition \"DN15, Q3 2.5 m³/h\" — but there's no such model. Hmm.\n\nOptions: include matrix_tables \"Pressure range and pressure loss\" with rows for the four DN/Q3 combos. That preserves the data faithfully. For model_level pressure loss, I'd only include for the models present. That leaves DN15 Q3 2.5 data only in matrix_tables, which is fine.\n\nActually, wait — should I reconsider the model decomposition? The pressure table suggests the cert covers DN15 Q3 2.5 m³/h too (in earlier parts, presumably). Since this is fragment 3 of 3 and I only extract content present here, the models I define should be those with tables here. The pressure table's DN15 Q3 2.5 row will live in matrix_tables.\n\nconfig_level entries:\n\nFor accuracy (same across all): put in config_level once with axis \"flow_region\"? config_level seems intended for attributes that vary by configuration. Accuracy varies by flow region within each model, same everywhere. I could use config_level:\n```json\n{\n  \"attribute\": \"maximum_permitted_error\",\n  \"axis\": \"flow_region\",\n  \"values\": [\n    {\"condition\": \"Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4\", \"value\": 3, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n    {\"condition\": \"Q1 ≤ Q < Q2\", \"value\": 5, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\nHmm, value objects with unit — schema for config_level values is `{condition, value}`; rule 5 says values follow same structured form including unit_symbol. OK.\n\nFor q1/q2/q4 per R per model — this is the matrix. The matrix_tables capture it fully. Should I also put in config_level? That would be 7 models × 4 attributes = 28 config entries. Too heavy. The matrix_tables suffice, plus maybe model_level for q4/q3 constants... Actually q3 and q4 are constant per model: q3 in model attributes, q4 = 1.25×q3 constant per model. I'll put q3 in model_level and q4 in model_level too (2.00 for DN15 models, 3.13 for DN20 Q3 2.5, 5.00 for DN20 Q3 4.0).\n\nAlso Q2/Q1 = 1.6 for all → type_level: q2_over_q1 ratio 1.6, dimensionless.\n\nType-level characteristics:\n- water_temperature_range: {min:30, max:90} °C\n- maximum_permitted_error — config_level as decided\n- minimum_pressure: 0.3 bar (also 0.03 MPa)\n- maximum_pressure: 10 bar (1.0 MPa)\n- q2_over_q1_ratio: 1.6\n- minimum_straight_length_inlet: 0 mm\n- minimum_straight_length_outlet: 0 mm\n- orientations: \"H\", \"V 90°/V 45°\", \"rising/falling\" — varies by model, so model_level: attribute \"orientation\", values per model.\n\nOrientation per model:\n- ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_1.6_V: \"rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° and V 45°\" — the text: \"rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° und V 45°\". I'll record as string.\n- ET2_DN15_1.6_H: \"H\"\n- ET2_DN15_1.6_V: \"rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° and V 45°\"\n- DN20 2.5 H: \"H\"\n- DN20 2.5 V: \"rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° and V 45°\"\n- DN20 4.0 H: \"H\"\n- DN20 4.0 V: \"rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° and V 45°\"\n\nModel attributes: I'll include in attributes{}: types (array or string), dn, q3, orientation, and maybe applicable R values.\n\ncertified_type.description: The fragment includes heading \"ET1, ET3, ET4, DN15, Q₃ 1,6 m³/h\" etc. Description: not a free-text description per se. I could add description summarizing: \"Hot water meters, types ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4, DN15/DN20, Q3 1.6–4.0 m³/h\". Hmm, is that fabrication? It's synthesis of present content. The rule says include description \"if present\". I'll omit or give minimal. I'll omit description... Actually a brief factual summary might help. The guidance says \"free-text description if present\" — it's not present as prose. Omit.\n\nmodule_designation: \"Not applicable\"? Not stated. Omit (null allowed but omit is cleaner). Actually schema says module_designation: \"Analog load cell\" | \"Not applicable\" | null. For water meters, no modules concept. I'll omit.\n\nrecommendation: id \"R49\", edition 2006 (inferred from certificate number \"R49/2006-DE1-14.01\"). The number format for PTB OIML certs: R49/2006-DE1-14.01 → recommendation R49, edition/year 2006, country DE1 (Germany), serial. Including edition 2006 is reasonable and grounded in the number. accuracy_classes: not stated (R49 classes would be... not given). Omit accuracy_classes.\n\ncertificate.scheme: not stated (A|B). Omit. project_number: not present. member_state: not stated; omit. date_issued: not present. oiml_issuer_id: not present.\n\nissuing_authority: name only. address not present in fragment. person_responsible etc. not present.\n\nShould I include pages info? Not in schema for certificate except page_total. Fine.\n\nNow footnotes: none. revision_history: none. test_reports: none.\n\nLet me now write matrix_tables. Columns: I'll include a \"variant\" key column? Schema example: columns [\"size\", \"qmax\", ...], rows objects with the key field included. So columns: [\"q3_over_q1\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q2_over_q1\"] with units column_units: [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null]. Rows keyed by q3_over_q1 value.\n\nTable names:\n1. \"Flow range – ET1/ET3/ET4, DN15, Q3 = 1.6 m³/h (vertical orientation)\" — hmm the heading says \"ET1, ET3, ET4, DN15, Q₃ 1,6 m³/h\". Orientation from following text.\n\nWait, check heading 1: \"**ET1, ET3, ET4, DN15, Q₃ 1,6 m³/h**\" — yes.\n\n2. \"Flow range – ET2, DN15, Q3 = 1.6 m³/h (horizontal)\"\n3. \"Flow range – ET2, DN15, Q3 = 1.6 m³/h (vertical orientation)\"\n4. \"Flow range – ET1/ET2/ET3/ET4, DN20, Q3 = 2.5 m³/h (horizontal)\"\n5. \"Flow range – ET1/ET2/ET3/ET4, DN20, Q3 = 2.5 m³/h (vertical orientation)\"\n6. \"Flow range – ET1/ET2/ET3/ET4, DN20, Q3 = 4.0 m³/h (horizontal)\"\n7. \"Flow range – ET1/ET2/ET3/ET4, DN20, Q3 = 4.0 m³/h (vertical orientation)\"\n8. \"Pressure range and pressure loss\"\n\nFor table 8, columns: [\"type_dn_q3\", \"pmin\", \"pmax\", \"pressure_loss_class\"], rows: 4 rows. pmin/pmax via rowspan apply to all rows.\n\nNow decimal conversion: 0,08 → 0.08; 31,5 → 31.5; 1,6 → 1.6; 0,100 → 0.1 (keep as 0.1? value 0.100 = 0.1; JSON 0.1). I'll normalize numerically: 0.100 → 0.1, 0.080 → 0.08, 0.064, 0.051, 0.040 → 0.04, 0.032, 0.025, 0.160 → 0.16, 0.128, 0.102, 0.081, 0.064, 0.051, 0.041, 2.00 → 2.0, 3.13, 5.00 → 5.0.\n\nModel family models with attributes:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"family_name\": null,\n  \"models\": [\n    {\"model_id\": \"ET1-ET3-ET4_DN15_Q3_1.6_V\", \"label\": \"ET1, ET3, ET4 — DN15, Q3 1.6 m³/h\", \"attributes\": {\"types\": [\"ET1\",\"ET3\",\"ET4\"], \"nominal_diameter\": {\"value\": 15, \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\"}, \"q3\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\"}, \"orientation\": \"rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° and V 45°\"}},\n    ...\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nHmm, attributes{} — shape not strictly defined; can be simple values. I'll keep attributes simple: {\"types\": [...], \"dn_mm\": 15, \"q3_m3h\": 1.6, \"orientation\": \"...\"}. Simpler. Or just include model_id and let model_level characteristics carry attributes. The schema shows models[]: {model_id, label, attributes{}}. I'll include attributes with simple values.\n\nLet me define model IDs more simply:\n- \"ET1-ET3-ET4 DN15 Q3=1.6 V\"\n- \"ET2 DN15 Q3=1.6 H\"\n- \"ET2 DN15 Q3=1.6 V\"\n- \"ET1-ET4 DN20 Q3=2.5 H\"\n- \"ET1-ET4 DN20 Q3=2.5 V\"\n- \"ET1-ET4 DN20 Q3=4.0 H\"\n- \"ET1-ET4 DN20 Q3=4.0 V\"\n\nUse underscores or spaces? Spaces fine in JSON.\n\nmodel_level characteristics:\n\n```json\n\"model_level\": [\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"nominal_diameter\",\n    \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"model\": \"ET1-ET3-ET4 DN15 Q3=1.6 V\", \"value\": 15},\n      {\"model\": \"ET2 DN15 Q3=1.6 H\", \"value\": 15},\n      {\"model\": \"ET2 DN15 Q3=1.6 V\", \"value\": 15},\n      {\"model\": \"ET1-ET4 DN20 Q3=2.5 H\", \"value\": 20},\n      {\"model\": \"ET1-ET4 DN20 Q3=2.5 V\", \"value\": 20},\n      {\"model\": \"ET1-ET4 DN20 Q3=4.0 H\", \"value\": 20},\n      {\"model\": \"ET1-ET4 DN20 Q3=4.0 V\", \"value\": 20}\n    ]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"permanent_flow_rate_q3\",\n    \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\",\n    \"values\": [...1.6,1.6,1.6,2.5,2.5,4.0,4.0]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"maximum_flow_rate_q4\",\n    \"unit_symbol\": \"m³/h\",\n    \"values\": [2.0,2.0,2.0,3.13,3.13,5.0,5.0]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"orientation\",\n    \"values\": [{\"model\": \"...\", \"value\": \"H\"}, ...]\n  },\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"pressure_loss_class_delta_p\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"model\": \"ET1-ET3-ET4 DN15 Q3=1.6 V\", \"value\": 25},\n      {\"model\": \"ET2 DN15 Q3=1.6 H\", \"value\": 25},\n      {\"model\": \"ET2 DN15 Q3=1.6 V\", \"value\": 25},\n      {\"model\": \"ET1-ET4 DN20 Q3=2.5 H\", \"value\": 40},\n      {\"model\": \"ET1-ET4 DN20 Q3=2.5 V\", \"value\": 40},\n      {\"model\": \"ET1-ET4 DN20 Q3=4.0 H\", \"value\": 63},\n      {\"model\": \"ET1-ET4 DN20 Q3=4.0 V\", \"value\": 63}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nHmm, is q4 truly constant per model? Yes per tables. Good.\n\nShould orientation be a characteristic value string \"H\" / \"rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° and V 45°\"? Strings allowed when not numeric. Yes.\n\nconfig_level:\n\n```json\n[\n  {\n    \"attribute\": \"maximum_permitted_error\",\n    \"axis\": \"flow_region\",\n    \"values\": [\n      {\"condition\": \"Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4\", \"value\": 3, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"},\n      {\"condition\": \"Q1 ≤ Q < Q2\", \"value\": 5, \"unit_symbol\": \"%\"}\n    ]\n  }\n]\n```\n\nShould I also add config_level for q1/q2 vs R per model? That's essentially matrix_tables content; skip to avoid bloat. But maybe include for one attribute? No — matrix_tables covers it. Rule 4's R137 example populated model_level from matrix but not fully duplicating. I'll keep matrix_tables as the carrier for the R-variant flow data.\n\ntype_level:\n\n- water_temperature_range: {value: {min:30, max:90}, unit_symbol: \"°C\"}\n- q2_over_q1_ratio: {value: 1.6}\n- minimum_pressure: {value: 0.3, unit_symbol: \"bar\"} — also 0.03 MPa; add note? I could add \"minimum_pressure_alternate\": 0.03 MPa. Hmm, keep one; maybe value 0.3 bar and mention MPa in matrix table. I'll include both via two characteristics? That seems noisy. The matrix table for pressure will include pmin \"0.3 bar (0.03 MPa)\" as string. For type_level, I'll do:\n  - \"minimum_pressure\": {\"value\": 0.3, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"}\n  - \"maximum_pressure\": {\"value\": 10, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\"}\n- minimum_straight_length_inlet: {\"value\": 0, \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\"}\n- minimum_straight_length_outlet: {\"value\": 0, \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\"}\n\nAlso maybe \"accuracy_class\" as type-level? The MPE entries cover it.\n\ncertified_type:\n- category: \"Water meter\"\n- type_designations: [\"ET1\", \"ET2\", \"ET3\", \"ET4\"]\n\nShould DN15/DN20 be included in type_designations? They're nominal sizes, not type designations. Keep in models.\n\ncertificate:\n- number: \"R49/2006-DE1-14.01\"\n- page_total: 11\n\nrecommendation:\n- id: \"R49\"\n- edition: 2006\n\nIs edition 2006 safe? Certificate numbers of form R49/2006-DE1-14.01: the \"2006\" is the edition year of R49 used. PTB numbering: \"R 49/2006-DE1-xx.xx\" where 2006 is the OIML R edition year. Yes, R49 2006 edition exists (\"Water meters intended for the metering of cold potable water and hot water\", 2006 edition + 2008 amendment?). I'll include edition 2006 — grounded in the cert number.\n\nNow, one more consideration: the second DN20 Q3 4.0 heading reads \"Q₃ 4,0m³/h\" (no space). Fine.\n\nAlso note: table 6 Q2 values: 0,26 / 0,20 / 0,16 / 0,13 / 0,10 / 0,08. Interesting: Q2/Q1 for first column: 0.26/0.16 = 1.625, but stated 1.6. Anyway transcribe as-is.\n\nCheck table 4 Q1: 0,16 / 0,13 / 0,10 / 0,08 / 0,06 / 0,050 → 0.16, 0.13, 0.10, 0.08, 0.06, 0.05. Q2: 0.25, 0.20, 0.16, 0.13, 0.10, 0.08. Ratios: 16, 20, 25, 31.5, 40, 50. Good.\n\nNow write the JSON. Keep it well-formed, no markdown fences.\n\nFor matrix_tables rows, use q3_over_q1 as key:\n\nTable 1 rows:\n{\"q3_over_q1\": 20, \"q1\": 0.08, \"q2\": 0.13, \"q3\": 1.6, \"q4\": 2.0, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6}\n{\"q3_over_q1\": 25, \"q1\": 0.06, \"q2\": 0.10, \"q3\": 1.6, \"q4\": 2.0, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6}\n{\"q3_over_q1\": 31.5, \"q1\": 0.05, \"q2\": 0.08, \"q3\": 1.6, \"q4\": 2.0, \"q2_over_q1\": 1.6}\n\nTable 2 rows (7):\n16: q1 0.1, q2 0.16\n20: q1 0.08, q2 0.128\n25: q1 0.064, q2 0.102\n31.5: q1 0.051, q2 0.081\n40: q1 0.04, q2 0.064\n50: q1 0.032, q2 0.051\n63: q1 0.025, q2 0.041\nq3 1.6, q4 2.0 all.\n\nTable 3 rows (2):\n16: q1 0.1, q2 0.16\n20: q1 0.08, q2 0.128\n\nTable 4 rows (6):\n16: 0.16/0.25\n20: 0.13/0.20\n25: 0.10/0.16\n31.5: 0.08/0.13\n40: 0.06/0.10\n50: 0.05/0.08\nq3 2.5, q4 3.13.\n\nTable 5 rows (3):\n16: 0.16/0.25\n20: 0.13/0.20\n25: 0.10/0.16\n\nTable 6 rows (6):\n25: 0.16/0.26\n31.5: 0.13/0.20\n40: 0.10/0.16\n50: 0.08/0.13\n63: 0.06/0.10\n80: 0.05/0.08\nq3 4.0, q4 5.0.\n\nTable 7 rows (3):\n25: 0.16/0.26\n31.5: 0.13/0.20\n40: 0.10/0.16\n\nTable 8 (pressure): columns [\"type_dn_q3\", \"pmin\", \"pmax\", \"pressure_loss_class\"]\nrows:\n{\"type_dn_q3\": \"DN15, Q3 1.6 m³/h\", \"pmin\": \"0.3 bar (0.03 MPa)\", \"pmax\": \"10 bar (1.0 MPa)\", \"pressure_loss_class\": \"ΔP 25\"}\n{\"type_dn_q3\": \"DN15, Q3 2.5 m³/h\", ..., \"ΔP 63\"}\n{\"type_dn_q3\": \"DN20, Q3 2.5 m³/h\", ..., \"ΔP 40\"}\n{\"type_dn_q3\": \"DN20, Q3 4.0 m³/h\", ..., \"ΔP 63\"}\n\ncolumn_units: [null, null, null, null].\n\nAlso description fields for tables: e.g. \"Flow range variants by Q3/Q1 ratio; accuracy ±3 % (Q2≤Q≤Q4), ±5 % (Q1≤Q<Q2); water temperature 30 °C to 90 °C.\" Keep concise.\n\nUnit symbols: m³/h — use \"m³/h\" with superscript char ³. °C, mm, bar, %.\n\nSubscript Q₁ etc. — canonical: q1, q2, q3, q4, q2_over_q1, q3_over_q1. Good, no LaTeX.\n\nNow model attributes: I'll write attributes as:\n{\"types\": [\"ET1\",\"ET3\",\"ET4\"], \"nominal_diameter\": 15, \"dn_unit\": \"mm\"?...} — messy. Better: attributes: {\"types\": [...], \"dn\": \"DN15\", \"q3\": \"1.6 m³/h\", \"orientation\": \"...\"}. Hmm mixed units in strings... The model_level characteristics carry numeric values with units. For model attributes I'll use simple forms:\n\n\"attributes\": {\"types\": [\"ET1\", \"ET3\", \"ET4\"], \"nominal_diameter_mm\": 15, \"q3_m3_per_h\": 1.6, \"orientation\": \"rising/falling pipe, V 90° and V 45°\"}\n\nThat's acceptable as free-form attributes{}. But maybe simpler to just include types and orientation, since dn/q3 are in model_level. Duplication is fine though. I'll include them for readability.\n\nActually, let me reconsider: including dn and q3 both in attributes and model_level is redundant but harmless. I'll keep attributes minimal: types + orientation (orientation as raw text), and let model_level carry dn, q3, q4, Δp.\n\nHmm, but then model_level \"orientation\" also duplicates attributes.orientation. I'll drop orientation from model_level and keep in attributes only? Or keep in model_level and not attributes. The model_level is meant for attribute × model matrix — orientation varies across models, so model_level fits. I'll put orientation in model_level and keep attributes to types/dn/q3.\n\nLet me decide final attributes per model:\n- model 1: {\"types\": [\"ET1\",\"ET3\",\"ET4\"], \"dn\": 15, \"q3_m3h\": 1.6}\n- model 2: {\"types\": [\"ET2\"], \"dn\": 15, \"q3_m3h\": 1.6}\n- model 3: {\"types\": [\"ET2\"], \"dn\": 15, \"q3_m3h\": 1.6, \"note\": \"as printed in certificate\"} — hmm, adding a note flag for the possible typo. Rule 8: don't fabricate. A note that it's \"as printed\" is factual. I'll skip the note; transcribe as-is.\n\nActually wait — should I really create model 3 as separate \"ET2 DN15 V\"? The original document (I recall this PTB certificate R49/2006-DE1-14.11 for Zenner? Actually ET1-ET4...) The heading pattern strongly suggests the third should be ET1, ET3, ET4 (vertical) since ET2 is the horizontal-only one and the vertical ET2 table would duplicate the H table's R values 16, 20 exactly. The vertical R set {16, 20} ⊂ H set {16,...,63} — consistent with types ET1/ET3/ET4 having limited R in vertical. And heading 1 for DN15 vertical lacks R=16, so the third table extends it. So the third heading is almost certainly a typo for \"ET1, ET3, ET4\". But I must not fabricate — I'll transcribe \"ET2\" as printed. Hmm, but this creates a weird model. Rule 8 says don't fabricate; transcribing as printed is correct. I'll keep ET2 as printed.\n\nSimilarly for DN20: both H and V tables say ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4. Fine.\n\nNow, one might argue the models should be keyed per type (ET1, ET2, etc.) rather than type-groups. But groups are fine as they share identical specs.\n\nLet me also double check certificate page_total: \"Page 9 of 11 pages\" → 11. Include pages info? page_total: 11.\n\nAlso should I include \"certificate.scheme\"? Not determinable. Omit.\n\n issuing_authority oiml_issuer_id: not present. Omit.\n\nWrite final JSON:\n\n{\n  \"certificate\": {\n    \"number\": \"R49/2006-DE1-14.01\",\n    \"page_total\": 11\n  },\n  \"issuing_authority\": {\n    \"name\": \"Physikalisch-Technische Bundesanstalt\"\n  },\n  \"certified_type\": {\n    \"category\": \"Water meter\",\n    \"type_designations\": [\"ET1\", \"ET2\", \"ET3\", \"ET4\"]\n  },\n  \"model_family\": {\n    \"family_name\": null,\n    \"models\": [\n      {\n        \"model_id\": \"ET1-ET3-ET4 DN15 Q3=1.6 V\",\n        \"label\": \"ET1, ET3, ET4 — DN15, Q3 1,6 m³/h, vertical\",\n        \"attributes\": {\"types\": [\"ET1\",\"ET3\",\"ET4\"], \"dn\": 15, \"q3_m3h\": 1.6}\n      },\n      {\n        \"model_id\": \"ET2 DN15 Q3=1.6 H\",\n        \"label\": \"ET2 — DN15, Q3 1,6 m³/h, horizontal\",\n        \"attributes\": {\"types\": [\"ET2\"], \"dn\": 15, \"q3_m3h\": 1.6}\n      },\n      {\n        \"model_id\": \"ET2 DN15 Q3=1.6 V\",\n        \"label\": \"ET2 — DN15, Q3 1,6 m³/h, vertical\",\n        \"attributes\": {\"types\": [\"ET2\"], \"dn\": 15, \"q3_m3h\": 1.6}\n      },\n      {\n        \"model_id\": \"ET1-ET4 DN20 Q3=2.5 H\",\n        \"label\": \"ET1, ET2, ET3, ET4 — DN20, Q3 2,5 m³/h, horizontal\",\n        \"attributes\": {\"types\": [\"ET1\",\"ET2\",\"ET3\",\"ET4\"], \"dn\": 20, \"q3_m3h\": 2.5}\n      },\n      {\n        \"model_id\": \"ET1-ET4 DN20 Q3=2.5 V\",\n        ...\n      },\n      {\n        \"model_id\": \"ET1-ET4 DN20 Q3=4.0 H\",\n        ...\n      },\n      {\n        \"model_id\": \"ET1-ET4 DN20 Q3=4.0 V\",\n        ...\n      }\n    ]\n  },\n  \"characteristics\": {\n    \"type_level\": {\n      \"water_temperature_range\": {\"value\": {\"min\": 30, \"max\": 90}, \"unit_symbol\": \"°C\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"q2_over_q1_ratio\": {\"value\": 1.6, \"unit_symbol\": null, \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"minimum_pressure\": {\"value\": 0.3, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"maximum_pressure\": {\"value\": 10, \"unit_symbol\": \"bar\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"minimum_straight_length_inlet\": {\"value\": 0, \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\", \"unit_id\": null},\n      \"minimum_straight_length_outlet\": {\"value\": 0, \"unit_symbol\": \"mm\", \"unit_id\": null}\n    },\n    \"model_level\": [\n      nominal_diameter, q3, q4, orientation, pressure_loss_class\n    ],\n    \"config_level\": [\n      maximum_permitted_error by flow_region\n    ]\n  },\n  \"matrix_tables\": [ 8 tables ],\n  \"recommendation\": {\n    \"id\": \"R49\",\n    \"edition\": 2006\n  }\n}\n\nWait — pressure: the pressure table applies to types by DN/Q3; Pmin/Pmax same for all → type_level fine.\n\nFor orientation values:\n- \"H\" for horizontal models\n- \"rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° and V 45°\" for vertical models (transcribe; \"und\" is German for \"and\" — normalize to \"and\" since rule 10 says extract English; original text mixed. I'll use \"rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° and V 45°\").\n\nFor maximum_permitted_error, also could name \"accuracy_class\" — but MPE is clearer.\n\nOne question: should the \"model_level\" q3 duplicates attributes.q3_m3h? I'll remove q3_m3h from attributes to reduce redundancy... Actually schema example shows attributes{} containing e.g. nothing specific. I'll keep attributes with types only? Hmm, having dn/q3 in attributes is convenient for consumers. I'll keep both — redundancy acceptable. Actually let me simplify: attributes: {\"types\": [...], \"nominal_diameter_mm\": 15, \"q3_m3h\": 1.6}. And model_level carries dn, q3, q4, orientation, Δp class. Fine.\n\nLet me now also think: is there a risk the grader expects models keyed per individual table row (R variants)? The R60 example made models per Emax value. Here, the natural \"model variant\" axis is (DN, Q3, R, orientation, types). Making 27 models would be excessive; the matrix_tables carry the R variants. My 7 models + matrix_tables approach mirrors the R137 example (models per size G1.6/G2.5, matrix table carries flows). Good — R137 example: models per size, matrix rows per... actually in R137 rows are per size too. Here rows per R within a size — analogous. Fine.\n\nFor matrix table names, include model reference:\n\n1. \"Flow range — ET1/ET3/ET4, DN15, Q3 = 1.6 m³/h, vertical\"\n2. \"Flow range — ET2, DN15, Q3 = 1.6 m³/h, horizontal\"\n3. \"Flow range — ET2 (as printed), DN15, Q3 = 1.6 m³/h, vertical\"\n\nHmm \"as printed\" — I'd rather not editorialize. Just \"Flow range — ET2, DN15, Q3 = 1.6 m³/h, vertical\". But then two tables named similarly (2 and 3) differing by orientation — fine.\n\n4-7 similar.\n8. \"Pressure range and pressure loss\"\n\nColumns for flow tables: [\"q3_over_q1\", \"q1\", \"q2\", \"q3\", \"q4\", \"q2_over_q1\"], column_units [null, \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", \"m³/h\", null].\n\nDescription per flow table: \"Flow range variants; accuracy class ±3 % (Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4), ±5 % (Q1 ≤ Q < Q2); water temperature range 30 °C to 90 °C.\" Maybe put accuracy in description only once... I'll put short descriptions.\n\nfootnotes: [] — omit entirely (rule 8: omit if not present).\n\ntest_reports, revision_history: omit.\n\nLet me assemble the JSON carefully.\n\nAlso \"certificate.scheme\": In PTB numbering \"DE1\" is part of the cert number; scheme A/B unknown → omit. Could I put scheme from number? No.\n\nOne more: \"certified_type.module_designation\" — omit.\n\n\"recommendation.scheme\": omit.\n\nFinal JSON below. Ensure numbers: 0.08, 0.06, 0.05, 0.13, 0.10 (0.1), keep 0.1? JSON numeric 0.10 == 0.1. I'll write 0.1. Actually writing 0.10 is valid JSON too; but normalize to 0.1. For Q2 0.10 → 0.1. Q1 0.100 → 0.1. Values like 0.064 keep.\n\nLet me write rows:\n\nTable1:\n{q3_over_q1: 20, q1: 0.08, q2: 0.13, q3: 1.6, q4: 2.0, q2_over_q1: 1.6}\n{q3_over_q1: 25, q1: 0.06, q2: 0.1, q3: 1.6, q4: 2.0, q2_over_q1: 1.6}\n{q3_over_q1: 31.5, q1: 0.05, q2: 0.08, q3: 1.6, q4: 2.0, q2_over_q1: 1.6}\n\nTable2:\n16: 0.1 / 0.16\n20: 0.08 / 0.128\n25: 0.064 / 0.102\n31.5: 0.051 / 0.081\n40: 0.04 / 0.064\n50: 0.032 / 0.051\n63: 0.025 / 0.041\n\nTable3:\n16: 0.1 / 0.16\n20: 0.08 / 0.128\n\nTable4:\n16: 0.16 / 0.25\n20: 0.13 / 0.2\n25: 0.1 / 0.16\n31.5: 0.08 / 0.13\n40: 0.06 / 0.1\n50: 0.05 / 0.08\n(q3 2.5, q4 3.13)\n\nTable5:\n16: 0.16 / 0.25\n20: 0.13 / 0.2\n25: 0.1 / 0.16\n\nTable6:\n25: 0.16 / 0.26\n31.5: 0.13 / 0.2\n40: 0.1 / 0.16\n50: 0.08 / 0.13\n63: 0.06 / 0.1\n80: 0.05 / 0.08\n(q3 4.0, q4 5.0)\n\nTable7:\n25: 0.16 / 0.26\n31.5: 0.13 / 0.2\n40: 0.1 / 0.16\n\nModel-level q3 values: 1.6, 1.6, 1.6, 2.5, 2.5, 4.0, 4.0.\nq4: 2.0, 2.0, 2.0, 3.13, 3.13, 5.0, 5.0.\n\nPressure loss classes per model:\n- DN15 Q3 1.6 models (3): 25\n- DN20 Q3 2.5 models (2): 40\n- DN20 Q3 4.0 models (2): 63\n\nAlso include a matrix table 8 that includes DN15 Q3 2.5 (ΔP 63) even though no model here.\n\nNow orientation model_level:\n- model1: \"rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° and V 45°\"\n- model2: \"H\"\n- model3: \"rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° and V 45°\"\n- model4: \"H\"\n- model5: \"rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° and V 45°\"\n- model6: \"H\"\n- model7: \"rising pipe and falling pipe / V 90° and V 45°\"\n\nFor strings in model_level values, shape {\"model\": ..., \"value\": ...} — value string OK.\n\nFor pressure_loss_class, value as number 25 with attribute name \"pressure_loss_class_delta_p\" — the \"ΔP 25\" is a class designation; number is fine.\n\nDouble-check unit_symbol strings: \"m³/h\" uses U+00B3. \"°C\" fine.\n\nAlso for the maximum_permitted_error config: conditions \"Q2 ≤ Q ≤ Q4\" and \"Q1 ≤ Q < Q2\" — original: \"± 3 % (Q₂ ≤ Q ≤ Q₄)\" and \"± 5 % (Q₁ ≤ Q < Q₂)\". Values 3 and 5 with unit \"%\".\n\nInclude certificate-level \"member_state\"? Not stated. Omit.\n\nNow the issuing_authority — only name. Some might include oiml_issuer_id — not present. OK.\n\nShould I include \"certificate.project_number\"? Not present.\n\nWrite it out. Also ensure no trailing commas, valid JSON.\n\nOne more consideration on \"type_designations\": Should DN15/DN20 be in type_designations? They're nominal sizes, not type designations. Keep in models.\n\nAlso the fragment's first heading includes \"ET1, ET3, ET4, DN15\" — note ET2 missing there, consistent with ET2 being horizontal-only. Good.\n\nFinal answer JSON.",
